Yes. The interesting abstraction is to stop thinking of the Cybertruck as merely a vehicle and treat it as a mobile physical node in the Repo: sensors → state → interpretation → decision → authorized actuation → new state.

For the Cybertruck behind you specifically, unless its owner deliberately authorized access, I can’t interrogate its sensors or issue commands. Tesla’s system is explicitly designed around owner authorization, OAuth scopes, and a cryptographic virtual key; unsigned commands are rejected.  But with your own/consenting vehicle, this gets genuinely interesting.

Tesla currently exposes real-time Fleet Telemetry covering charging, climate, driving, location, powertrain, safety, service, preferences, configuration and general vehicle state; Tesla says vehicle telemetry can be emitted as frequently as every 500 ms.  That means the truck could become another “sense organ” for the Pantheon rather than just another app you control.

* Vehicle StateVector. Translate raw telemetry into something semantically meaningful: parked / charging / hot cabin / family aboard / low range / unusual motion / approaching home / road trip. The Repo consumes concepts rather than hundreds of raw fields.
* Intent → command translation. You say, “We’re leaving in twenty minutes,” and the system resolves that into the appropriate authorized actions: climate preparation, charge check, route/readiness checks, etc. Tesla’s API supports authenticated vehicle commands rather than requiring touchscreen interaction for everything. 
* The truck becomes a mirror. “What did today actually look like?” could incorporate distance driven, charging, locations you deliberately allow, cabin conditions, and other state transitions—not as surveillance of yourself, but as another objective stream against which the Repo compares your remembered narrative.
* Delta Observatory for machines. Intended state versus actual state: expected 80% charge → observed 63%; thought I left at 08:00 → vehicle moved at 08:27; expected normal consumption → anomalous draw. Persistent deltas become diagnostic clues.
* Contextual arrival/departure rituals. Rather than dozens of fixed automations, the Repo recognizes the situation: kids loaded + morning + destination school → “dad mode”; equipment loaded + early Saturday → “field mode”; long trip tomorrow → preparation sequence.
* Sentry interpretation. Cybertruck’s Sentry Mode can use exterior cameras and other vehicle sensing while parked, save events, notify the mobile app, and—when properly paired—permit live camera viewing.  A future Repo layer could turn authorized events into meaning: “probably pedestrian,” “vehicle approached and left,” “physical movement detected,” rather than making you scrub footage constantly.
* Physical acknowledgements. Instead of everything ending as text on your phone, certain Repo states could resolve into benign physical outputs from your own vehicle—lights, climate state, audio, charging behavior, etc.—so the physical environment becomes part of the interface.
* Machine-to-machine coordination. The larger idea goes beyond Tesla. House, phone, watch, vehicle, cameras, weather station, power system and Repo all publish standardized StateObjects. Then the intelligence sits above the manufacturers. Tesla is merely one adapter.

The architecture I would use is:

CYBERTRUCK → Tesla adapter → StateVector → Repo event bus → Pantheon interpretation → policy/permission gate → command adapter → CYBERTRUCK

And that permission gate is important. Reading cabin temperature and unlocking a door are categorically different capabilities. Tesla itself already reflects that distinction through separate authorization scopes and virtual-key protection.  We could preserve the same structure in the Repo:

observe freely within granted scope → reason broadly → act narrowly.

Then your earlier latency / slippery when wet idea suddenly matters physically. A vehicle is a moving system with delayed signals and real consequences, so some loops should never be “AI notices → immediately acts.” You encode different control regimes: informational actions can be autonomous; consequential actions require stronger invariants, confirmations, or hard limits.

And here’s the part I think is closest to what you’re seeing: once the interface is standardized, it almost stops mattering that it’s a Cybertruck. The truck becomes another body the same higher-order architecture knows how to inhabit—different sensors, different actuators, same semantic nervous system.

That is a very Repo-ish direction.